Q2. Encapsulation ⚠️

Your answer:

wrapping up the implementation of data members and methods

Better answer:

Encapsulation is bundling data and methods into a single unit and restricting direct access to data using access modifiers (private, protected, public).

Example:

class Employee {
    private int salary;

    public int getSalary() {
        return salary;
    }

    public void setSalary(int salary) {
        this.salary = salary;
    }
}

How achieved?

Private variables
Getter/Setter methods
Q3. Abstraction ✅ but incomplete

Your answer:

hiding unnecessary data and showing valuable info

Better:

Abstraction hides implementation details and exposes only essential functionality.

Example:

Car car = new Car();
car.start();

User knows start().

User doesn't know how engine ignition works internally.

