What will be the output?

This tests the difference between this() and super(), a very common interview question.

where is object?

Good question.

The object is created here:

Child c = new Child();

or even:

new Child();

The new keyword creates the object in memory.

For example:

Child c = new Child();
new Child() → creates the Child object
c → reference variable pointing to that object

Think of it like:

c  ------>  [ Child Object ]

Because Child extends Parent, the Child object contains both:

[ Parent Part ]
[ Child Part  ]

So when the object is created:

Memory for the whole Child object is allocated.
Parent constructor runs first to initialize the Parent part.
Child constructor runs to initialize the Child part.

That's why:

Child c = new Child();

prints:

Parent
Child

even though you never explicitly created a Parent object.

The Parent portion exists inside the Child object and must be initialized first.

Interviewer's follow-up

Is a separate Parent object created here?

Child c = new Child();

Answer:

❌ No.

Only one Child object is created.

The Parent constructor runs because the Child object contains the inherited Parent state, not because a separate Parent object is created.
